<!-- HERO -->
<section class="hero" id="top">
  <div class="container hero-inner">
    <p class="eyebrow">Rust CLI · .media.prompt schema 0.4 · multi-provider</p>
    <h1>Media generation you can commit.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      <strong>media-tool</strong> turns AI media generation into versioned config. A
      <code>.media.prompt</code> file declares the asset — type, quality tier, eval criteria,
      dependencies — and <code>generate-media-prompt</code> resolves the best provider,
      generates, grades, and falls back. No chat luck. Just files.
    </p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" href="/getting-started">Get started</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg" href="https://github.com/the-robot-lives/media-tool" rel="noopener">View on GitHub</a>
    </div>
    <p class="cta-fine"><em>Single Rust binary · no account · provider keys you already have</em></p>

    <div class="terminal" role="img" aria-label="Terminal session showing a media.prompt generation run">
{%raw}<div class="terminal-bar"><span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span><span class="terminal-title">zellij — assets/</span></div>
<pre><code>$ generate-media-prompt --dry-run --verbose assets/
plan: 4 prompts · 1 dependency tier · gemini:gemini-3.1-flash-image (medium)

$ generate-media-prompt -n 3 hero.media.prompt
hero.png ✓ eval 0.86 (pass 0.75) · hero.2.png ✓ 0.81 · hero.3.png ✓ 0.72
graded by eval criteria · best kept</code></pre>{/raw}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- BENEFITS (3-up) -->
<section class="section" id="why">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 class="visually-hidden">Why media-tool</h2>
    <div class="benefit-grid">
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Prompts are config, not chat luck</h3>
        <p>
          A <code>.media.prompt</code> file is YAML under schema 0.4: prompt text, negatives,
          output formats and dimensions, post-processing, tags. It lives in your repo, reviews
          like code, and diffs across commits.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Provider-portable by tier</h3>
        <p>
          Declare <code>quality: medium</code> instead of pinning a vendor. The CLI walks a
          candidate ladder — Gemini Imagen, Qwen Image, and more — and falls back to the next
          provider when one fails or grades poorly.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Built for agentic pipelines</h3>
        <p>
          Deterministic resolution, <code>--dry-run</code> plans, dependency DAGs with alias
          substitution, weighted eval criteria, and non-interactive
          exit codes. Safe to call from CI, scripts, and agents.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- FORMAT AT A GLANCE -->
<section class="section section-alt" id="format-glance">
  <div class="container split">
    <div class="split-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">The format</p>
      <h2>One file per asset. Real YAML.</h2>
      <p>
        No prompt-hosting service, no proprietary DSL — just a typed schema you can write by
        hand or emit from a script. Quality tiers drive provider selection; eval criteria make
        "good enough" a measurable number instead of a vibe.
      </p>
      <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/format">Read the format spec</a>
    </div>
    <div class="code-panel">
{%raw}<pre><code><span class="c"># logo.media.prompt</span>
schema: <span class="s">"0.4"</span>
id: logo-001
type: image
quality: medium   <span class="c"># low | medium | high</span>

prompt:
  text: <span class="s">"Minimal geometric logomark, blue hexagons on dark background"</span>
  negative: <span class="s">"realistic, photographic, text"</span>

output:
  formats:
    - format: png
  dimensions:
    width: 1024
    height: 1024
    aspect_ratio: <span class="s">"1:1"</span></code></pre>{/raw}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ASSET TYPES -->
<section class="section" id="assets">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Asset types</p>
    <h2>Images to video, songs to SVG</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">
      Binary types route to generation APIs; text types (SVG, Mermaid diagrams, HTML) route to
      text-capable chat providers and are written straight to disk.
    </p>
    <ul class="chip-row" role="list">
      <li class="chip">image</li>
      <li class="chip">music</li>
      <li class="chip">voice</li>
      <li class="chip">sfx</li>
      <li class="chip">video</li>
      <li class="chip">diagram</li>
      <li class="chip">svg</li>
      <li class="chip">html</li>
      <li class="chip">component</li>
    </ul>
    <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/providers">See the provider matrix</a>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- PIPELINE FIT -->
<section class="section section-alt" id="pipelines">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Pipeline fit</p>
    <h2>Deterministic enough to automate</h2>
    <div class="benefit-grid">
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Plan before you spend</h3>
        <p>
          <code>--dry-run --verbose</code> prints the resolved generation plan — providers,
          models, dependency tiers — without calling an API. Review it in a PR like any other
          build step.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Grade what you generate</h3>
        <p>
          Each prompt can carry an <code>eval</code> block: weighted criteria, a pass
          threshold, and reject-if rules. Outputs are graded and the tool falls back to the
          next provider until something passes.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Compose with dependencies</h3>
        <p>
          <code>depends_on</code> references let a hero banner embed a generated logo by alias
          into its prompt text. Tiers resolve in order; cycles abort loudly. Batch across
          zellij panes with <code>-j</code>.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="cta-row center">
      <a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" href="/getting-started">Generate your first asset</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
